# ─── Story vaults ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Test vaults live here; `pnpm vault:new <name>` creates one, and `mkdir -p`
# makes the directory, so nothing needs to be tracked to hold the place open.
#
# No exceptions. A whitelist here is a standing invitation to add "just one
# more" file, and a vault is the author's own writing — it does not belong in
# this repository under any name.
/vaults/

# `.litrpg/` is a vault's unambiguous signature. Ignoring it anywhere means a
# vault created outside /vaults/ still can't have its cache or config committed.
.litrpg/

# Guard against `litfire .` scaffolding a vault into the repo root.
#
# Every pattern below is ROOT-ANCHORED with a leading slash. That is
# load-bearing: this repo contains source/system/, source/themes/,
# source/ledger/, source/domain/, and source/vault/, and an unanchored
# `system/` would silently stop tracking real source code.
/raw/
/system/
/timeline/
/characters/
/themes/
/places/
/factions/
/situations/
/chapters/
/wiki/
/ledger/
/index.md
/log.md
/manuscript.md

# ─── Credentials ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Provider API keys normally live in ~/.config/litfire/credentials.json —
# outside this repo entirely. These patterns catch a credentials file that ends
# up in the working tree anyway, e.g. XDG_CONFIG_HOME pointed at the repo while
# testing, which is exactly the mistake that leaks a key.
credentials.json
.litfire/
litfire/credentials.json

.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa
id_ed25519

# ─── Editors and OS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# ─── Build and test artefacts ────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.tsbuildinfo
coverage/
.pnpm-store/

